select public.fd_ai_upsert_chunks('fd-textbook', '1b1a21163698', 5, $aim_json$[{"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:5.0#1", "source_key": "fd-textbook:5.0", "chapter": 5, "section": "5.0", "heading": "Overview", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 1, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — 5.0 Overview", "body": "Cox, J. C., Ingersoll, J. E., and Ross, S. A. (1981). The relation between forward prices and futures prices. Journal of Financial Economics, 9(4), 321–346.\nHull, J. C. (2022). Options, Futures, and Other Derivatives (11th ed.). Pearson. Chapter 5.\nKaldor, N. (1939). Speculation and economic stability. Review of Economic Studies, 7(1), 1–27.\nMcDonald, R. L. (2013). Derivatives Markets (3rd ed.). Pearson. Chapters 5–6.\nWorking, H. (1949). The theory of price of storage. American Economic Review, 39(6), 1254–1262."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:5.0#2", "source_key": "fd-textbook:5.0", "chapter": 5, "section": "5.0", "heading": "Decide first", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 2, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — 5.0 Decide first", "body": "⚙ Decision — choose first; the theory follows\nHow large a copper futures position should hedge a purchase of brass?\nNorthwind Components will buy 500,000 pounds of brass next month. No futures contract on brass exists, but brass is an alloy of copper and zinc, and its price moves closely, though not exactly, with the price of copper futures. From past data, the monthly change in the price of brass has a standard deviation of $0.040 per pound, the monthly change in the copper futures price has a standard deviation of $0.050 per pound, and the correlation between the two is 0.85. Select the hedge ratio: the number of pounds of copper futures to buy for each pound of brass to be purchased.\nThree results of the exercise organize this chapter. A hedge ratio of one, a pound of futures for each pound of exposure, is not the ratio that minimizes risk. The risk-minimizing ratio is 0.68, and Section 5.4 derives it. And even at the best ratio a substantial risk remains, because the asset hedged and the asset underlying the futures are not the same; this residual is basis risk, the subject of Section 5.3."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:5.1#3", "source_key": "fd-textbook:5.1", "chapter": 5, "section": "5.1", "heading": "Basic principles", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 3, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — 5.1 Basic principles", "body": "A hedge with futures is a position in futures chosen so that its gains and losses offset, as nearly as possible, the losses and gains on an existing exposure. A hedge that removes all risk is a perfect hedge. Perfect hedges are rare; this chapter concerns the construction of hedges that perform as closely to perfect as the available contracts permit.\nA short hedge is a hedge that involves a short position in futures. It is appropriate when the hedger already owns the asset and expects to sell it, or will come to own it: a farmer with a crop in the ground, an oil producer, an exporter that will receive foreign currency. If the price of the asset falls, the hedger loses on the sale of the asset and gains on the short futures. A long hedge involves a long position in futures. It is appropriate when the hedger will have to buy the asset: a manufacturer with a future requirement for a metal, an airline that will buy fuel, an importer that must pay in foreign currency. If the price rises, the hedger pays more for the asset and gains on the long futures."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:5.1#4", "source_key": "fd-textbook:5.1", "chapter": 5, "section": "5.1", "heading": "Basic principles", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 4, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — 5.1 Basic principles", "body": "The hedges of this chapter are of the kind sometimes called hedge-and-forget: the position is established at the outset and left unchanged until the hedge is lifted. Chapter 14 considers hedges that are adjusted continually."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:5.2#5", "source_key": "fd-textbook:5.2", "chapter": 5, "section": "5.2", "heading": "Arguments for and against hedging", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 5, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — 5.2 Arguments for and against hedging", "body": "The case for hedging is that most companies have no particular skill in predicting interest rates, exchange rates, or commodity prices, and are in business to manufacture, to sell, or to provide a service. By hedging these market variables a company can concentrate on the activities in which it does have an advantage and can avoid a sharp, unexpected fall in its cash flow, which may force it to cut investment or may threaten its solvency.\nThree arguments are made against hedging, and each has some force.\nShareholders can hedge for themselves. A shareholder with a diversified portfolio is already protected against many of the risks a single company faces: a portfolio that holds both a copper producer and a copper user has little exposure to the price of copper. The argument assumes that shareholders know as much as managers about the company's exposures, which they do not, and that they can hedge at the same cost, which small investors cannot. It also ignores the costs of financial distress, which fall on shareholders whether or not they are diversified."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:5.2#6", "source_key": "fd-textbook:5.2", "chapter": 5, "section": "5.2", "heading": "Arguments for and against hedging", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 6, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — 5.2 Arguments for and against hedging", "body": "Hedging may increase risk when competitors do not hedge. In an industry in which prices of the finished product adjust to the cost of raw materials, a company that does not hedge has a profit margin that is roughly constant. A company in that industry that fixes its raw-material cost while its selling price continues to vary has made its margin more variable. A hedging decision must take account of how the company's revenues respond to the variable being hedged.\nHedging can lead to a worse outcome. A hedge produces a loss on the futures in exactly the circumstances in which the unhedged position would have gained. The total outcome is as planned, but the futures loss is visible and may have to be explained to a board that does not examine both sides. For this reason a hedging policy should be approved in advance by the board and its purpose clearly communicated to management and shareholders.\nConcept check 5.1"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:5.2#7", "source_key": "fd-textbook:5.2", "chapter": 5, "section": "5.2", "heading": "Arguments for and against hedging", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 7, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — 5.2 Arguments for and against hedging", "body": "A jewellery manufacturer buys gold each month and sets its selling prices each month in line with the current price of gold, as do all its competitors. It is considering a long hedge that fixes the price of its gold purchases for the next year. What is the likely effect on the variability of its profit margin?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:5.3#8", "source_key": "fd-textbook:5.3", "chapter": 5, "section": "5.3", "heading": "Basis risk", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 8, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — 5.3 Basis risk", "body": "In the examples of Chapter 1 the hedge was exact: the asset, the quantity, and the date matched the contract. In practice three circumstances prevent this. The asset whose price is to be hedged may differ from the asset underlying the futures contract. The hedger may not know the exact date on which the asset will be bought or sold. And the hedge may have to be closed out before the delivery month of the contract. Each gives rise to basis risk.\nRecall from Section 2.2 that the basis is the spot price of the asset to be hedged less the futures price of the contract used:\n\\[ b=S-F. \\]\nSuppose that a hedge is established at time \\(t_1\\) and closed out at time \\(t_2\\), and let \\(S_1,F_1,b_1\\) and \\(S_2,F_2,b_2\\) be the spot price, futures price, and basis at the two dates. Consider a long hedge. The hedger buys the asset at \\(t_2\\) for \\(S_2\\) and has gained \\(F_2-F_1\\) on the futures. The effective price paid is\n\\[ S_2-(F_2-F_1)=F_1+(S_2-F_2)=F_1+b_2. \\]"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:5.3#9", "source_key": "fd-textbook:5.3", "chapter": 5, "section": "5.3", "heading": "Basis risk", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 9, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — 5.3 Basis risk", "body": "The same expression gives the effective price received under a short hedge. The futures price \\(F_1\\) is known when the hedge is established. The basis \\(b_2\\) at the date the hedge is lifted is not. The hedge has replaced uncertainty about the price \\(S_2\\) with uncertainty about the basis \\(b_2\\), which is ordinarily far smaller. If the asset hedged is the asset underlying the contract and the hedge is lifted at the maturity of the contract, \\(b_2=0\\) by convergence and the hedge is perfect."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:5.3#10", "source_key": "fd-textbook:5.3", "chapter": 5, "section": "5.3", "heading": "Basis risk", "kind": "example", "ord": 10, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — 5.3 Basis risk", "body": "Worked example 5.1 — A long hedge closed before maturity\nOn 1 March Northwind knows that it will buy 500,000 pounds of copper in mid-June. It buys 20 July copper futures at $4.10. On 15 June the spot price of copper is $4.32 and the July futures price is $4.28; Northwind closes the futures and buys the copper. Find the effective price.\n1\nGain on the futures. \\(F_2-F_1=4.28-4.10=\\$0.18\\) per pound.\n2\nBasis when the hedge is lifted. \\(b_2=S_2-F_2=4.32-4.28=\\$0.04\\).\n3\nEffective price. \\(S_2-(F_2-F_1)=4.32-0.18=\\$4.14\\), which equals \\(F_1+b_2=4.10+0.04\\).\n4\nTotal cost. \\(4.14\\times500{,}000=\\$2{,}070{,}000\\), compared with $2,160,000 unhedged. The cost differs from the $2,050,000 implied by the futures price by \\(0.04\\times500{,}000=\\$20{,}000\\), the effect of the basis."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:5.3#11", "source_key": "fd-textbook:5.3", "chapter": 5, "section": "5.3", "heading": "Choice of contract", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 11, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — 5.3 Choice of contract", "body": "Two choices affect basis risk. The first is the underlying asset. If no contract exists on the asset to be hedged, the hedger selects the contract whose futures price is most closely correlated with the price of that asset; this is a cross hedge, examined in the next section. The second is the delivery month. A contract that matures exactly when the hedge is to be lifted might seem the natural choice, but futures prices are sometimes erratic during the delivery month, and a long hedger who holds the contract into that month risks having to take physical delivery. The usual rule is therefore to choose the nearest delivery month that falls after the date on which the hedge will be lifted. Northwind's choice of the July contract for a hedge to be lifted in mid-June follows this rule.\nBasis risk can work in the hedger's favor or against it. For a long hedge, a basis at \\(t_2\\) that is lower than expected reduces the effective price paid and improves the hedger's position; for a short hedge the effect is reversed."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:5.4#12", "source_key": "fd-textbook:5.4", "chapter": 5, "section": "5.4", "heading": "Cross hedging and the minimum-variance hedge ratio", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 12, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — 5.4 Cross hedging and the minimum-variance hedge ratio", "body": "The hedge ratio is the size of the position taken in futures divided by the size of the exposure. When the asset underlying the futures is the asset being hedged, a hedge ratio of 1.0 is natural. When the two differ, a ratio of 1.0 is generally not the best choice, as the opening exercise showed. The hedger should choose the ratio that minimizes the variance of the value of the hedged position. Define\nTable 5.1 Notation for the minimum-variance hedge ratio\nSymbolMeaning\n\\(\\Delta S\\)Change in the spot price \\(S\\) of the asset hedged during the life of the hedge\n\\(\\Delta F\\)Change in the futures price \\(F\\) during the life of the hedge\n\\(\\sigma_S,\\ \\sigma_F\\)Standard deviations of \\(\\Delta S\\) and \\(\\Delta F\\)\n\\(\\rho\\)Coefficient of correlation between \\(\\Delta S\\) and \\(\\Delta F\\)\n\\(h^{*}\\)The hedge ratio that minimizes the variance of the hedged position\nResult 5.1 — Minimum-variance hedge ratio\n\\[ h^{*}=\\rho\\,\\frac{\\sigma_S}{\\sigma_F}. \\] With this ratio, the proportion of the variance of the unhedged position that the hedge removes, called the hedge effectiveness, is \\(\\rho^{2}\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:5.4#13", "source_key": "fd-textbook:5.4", "chapter": 5, "section": "5.4", "heading": "Cross hedging and the minimum-variance hedge ratio", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 13, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — 5.4 Cross hedging and the minimum-variance hedge ratio", "body": "The ratio \\(h^{*}\\) is the slope of the line of best fit when \\(\\Delta S\\) is regressed on \\(\\Delta F\\), and the hedge effectiveness is the \\(R^{2}\\) of that regression. In practice the parameters are estimated from historical data on changes in the two prices over non-overlapping intervals equal in length to the life of the hedge.\nFor the opening exercise, \\(h^{*}=0.85\\times0.040/0.050=0.68\\), and the hedge effectiveness is \\(0.85^{2}=0.7225\\). The standard deviation of the hedged cost is \\(\\sigma_S\\sqrt{1-\\rho^{2}}\\times500{,}000=0.040\\times0.5268\\times500{,}000=\\$10{,}536\\), against $20,000 unhedged. A hedge ratio of 1.0 gives $13,229: it overhedges, adding risk from the part of the copper price movement that is unrelated to brass."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:5.4#14", "source_key": "fd-textbook:5.4", "chapter": 5, "section": "5.4", "heading": "Cross hedging and the minimum-variance hedge ratio", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 14, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — 5.4 Cross hedging and the minimum-variance hedge ratio", "body": "★ Optional: proof of Result 5.1. Consider a hedger who is long one unit of the asset and short \\(h\\) units of futures; the argument for a long hedge is identical with the signs reversed. The change in the value of the position is \\(\\Delta S-h\\,\\Delta F\\), and its variance is \\[ v=\\sigma_S^{2}+h^{2}\\sigma_F^{2}-2h\\rho\\,\\sigma_S\\sigma_F . \\] Differentiating, \\(dv/dh=2h\\sigma_F^{2}-2\\rho\\,\\sigma_S\\sigma_F\\). Setting the derivative to zero gives \\(h^{*}=\\rho\\,\\sigma_S/\\sigma_F\\), and since \\(d^{2}v/dh^{2}=2\\sigma_F^{2}>0\\) the solution is a minimum. Substituting \\(h^{*}\\) into \\(v\\) gives \\(v^{*}=\\sigma_S^{2}(1-\\rho^{2})\\), so the proportion of variance removed is \\(1-v^{*}/\\sigma_S^{2}=\\rho^{2}\\). \\(\\blacksquare\\)"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:5.4#15", "source_key": "fd-textbook:5.4", "chapter": 5, "section": "5.4", "heading": "The optimal number of contracts", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 15, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — 5.4 The optimal number of contracts", "body": "Let \\(Q_A\\) be the size of the position being hedged, in units of the asset, and \\(Q_F\\) the size of one futures contract. The number of contracts is\n\\[ N^{*}=\\frac{h^{*}\\,Q_A}{Q_F}. \\]\nBecause futures are settled daily, a hedge held for more than a few days is more accurately constructed by matching daily percentage changes in the two prices. If \\(\\hat\\sigma_S\\) and \\(\\hat\\sigma_F\\) are the standard deviations of the daily percentage changes, \\(\\hat\\rho\\) their correlation, and \\(\\hat h=\\hat\\rho\\,\\hat\\sigma_S/\\hat\\sigma_F\\), then\n\\[ N^{*}=\\frac{\\hat h\\,V_A}{V_F}, \\]\nwhere \\(V_A=S\\,Q_A\\) is the dollar value of the position hedged and \\(V_F=F\\,Q_F\\) is the dollar value of one futures contract. This adjustment is called tailing the hedge. The number of contracts then changes as \\(V_A\\) and \\(V_F\\) change, though in practice the changes are small."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:5.4#16", "source_key": "fd-textbook:5.4", "chapter": 5, "section": "5.4", "heading": "The optimal number of contracts", "kind": "example", "ord": 16, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — 5.4 The optimal number of contracts", "body": "Worked example 5.2 — An airline's cross hedge\nAn airline will buy 2,000,000 gallons of jet fuel in one month and hedges with heating oil futures, each on 42,000 gallons. Over one month, \\(\\sigma_S=0.030\\) and \\(\\sigma_F=0.036\\) dollars per gallon and \\(\\rho=0.90\\). (a) Find the hedge ratio, the number of contracts, and the hedge effectiveness. (b) If the same parameters describe daily percentage changes, the spot price of jet fuel is $2.60, and the futures price is $2.50, find the number of contracts when the hedge is tailed.\n1\nHedge ratio. \\(h^{*}=0.90\\times0.030/0.036=0.75\\).\n2\nContracts. \\(N^{*}=0.75\\times2{,}000{,}000/42{,}000=35.7\\). The airline buys 36 contracts; it is a buyer of fuel, so the hedge is long.\n3\nEffectiveness. \\(\\rho^{2}=0.81\\). The hedge removes 81 percent of the variance of the cost of the fuel; the standard deviation falls to \\(\\sqrt{1-0.81}=43.6\\) percent of its unhedged value.\n4\n(b) Tailing. \\(V_A=2{,}000{,}000\\times2.60=\\$5{,}200{,}000\\); \\(V_F=42{,}000\\times2.50=\\$105{,}000\\). \\(N^{*}=0.75\\times5{,}200{,}000/105{,}000=37.1\\), or 37 contracts."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:5.4#17", "source_key": "fd-textbook:5.4", "chapter": 5, "section": "5.4", "heading": "The optimal number of contracts", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 17, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — 5.4 The optimal number of contracts", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 5.1 — Three-dimensional view\nThe risk of a hedged position as a function of the hedge ratio and the correlation\nThe surface shows the standard deviation of the hedged position, as a multiple of the unhedged standard deviation, when spot and futures prices are equally volatile. For each correlation the surface has a valley, and the floor of the valley lies at \\(h=\\rho\\). The valley reaches zero only when the correlation is 1. Drag to rotate; scroll or pinch to zoom.\nTable equivalent of this figure"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:5.4#18", "source_key": "fd-textbook:5.4", "chapter": 5, "section": "5.4", "heading": "The optimal number of contracts", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 18, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — 5.4 The optimal number of contracts", "body": "Concept check 5.2\nThe correlation between changes in the spot price of an asset and changes in a futures price is 0.60. With the minimum-variance hedge ratio, by what proportion is the standard deviation of the position reduced?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:5.5#19", "source_key": "fd-textbook:5.5", "chapter": 5, "section": "5.5", "heading": "Stock index futures", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 19, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — 5.5 Stock index futures", "body": "Stock index futures are used to hedge well-diversified portfolios of shares. The relevant measure of the relation between a portfolio and the market is its beta, denoted \\(\\beta\\): the slope of the line of best fit when the return on the portfolio in excess of the risk-free rate is regressed on the excess return of the market index. A portfolio with a beta of 1.0 tends to move with the index; a portfolio with a beta of 1.3 tends to move 1.3 percent, in excess return, for each 1 percent excess return on the index. Beta plays the role of the hedge ratio \\(\\hat h\\) in the tailed formula of Section 5.4.\nResult 5.2 — Hedging an equity portfolio\nTo hedge a portfolio of value \\(V_A\\) and beta \\(\\beta\\), take a short position in \\[ N^{*}=\\beta\\,\\frac{V_A}{V_F} \\] index futures contracts, where \\(V_F\\) is the futures price multiplied by the contract multiplier. To change the beta of the portfolio from \\(\\beta\\) to \\(\\beta^{*}\\), take a short position in \\((\\beta-\\beta^{*})V_A/V_F\\) contracts if \\(\\beta>\\beta^{*}\\), or a long position in \\((\\beta^{*}-\\beta)V_A/V_F\\) contracts if \\(\\beta^{*}>\\beta\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:5.5#20", "source_key": "fd-textbook:5.5", "chapter": 5, "section": "5.5", "heading": "Stock index futures", "kind": "example", "ord": 20, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — 5.5 Stock index futures", "body": "Worked example 5.3 — Hedging a pension portfolio\nThe Northwind pension fund holds an equity portfolio worth $12,000,000 with a beta of 1.3. The index stands at 6,000, the three-month index futures price is 6,042, and each contract is on $50 times the index. The risk-free rate is 1 percent per quarter and the dividend yield on the index is 0.3 percent per quarter. (a) Find the hedge. (b) Verify its performance if the index falls by 10 percent in three months. (c) What position reduces the beta to 0.6?\n1\n(a) \\(V_F=6{,}042\\times50=\\$302{,}100\\). \\(N^{*}=1.3\\times12{,}000{,}000/302{,}100=51.6\\); the fund sells 52 contracts.\n2\n(b) The portfolio. The index returns \\(-10\\%+0.3\\%=-9.7\\%\\) including dividends. The expected return on the portfolio is \\(1\\%+1.3\\times(-9.7\\%-1\\%)=-12.91\\%\\). Its value falls to \\(12{,}000{,}000\\times0.8709=\\$10{,}450{,}800\\).\n3\nThe futures. At maturity the futures price equals the index, 5,400. Gain on the short position: \\(52\\times(6{,}042-5{,}400)\\times50=\\$1{,}669{,}200\\).\n4"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:5.5#21", "source_key": "fd-textbook:5.5", "chapter": 5, "section": "5.5", "heading": "Stock index futures", "kind": "example", "ord": 21, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — 5.5 Stock index futures", "body": "The hedged position. \\(10{,}450{,}800+1{,}669{,}200=\\$12{,}120{,}000\\), a return of 1.0 percent. The hedged portfolio earns the risk-free rate: by removing the market risk, the fund has in effect converted the portfolio into a risk-free investment for the quarter.\n5\n(c) \\((1.3-0.6)\\times12{,}000{,}000/302{,}100=27.8\\); the fund sells 28 contracts."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:5.5#22", "source_key": "fd-textbook:5.5", "chapter": 5, "section": "5.5", "heading": "Stock index futures", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 22, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — 5.5 Stock index futures", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 5.2 — Hedging an equity portfolio\nThe hedged portfolio earns the risk-free rate whatever the market does\nThe figure reproduces Worked example 5.3 for any beta and any change in the index. The number of contracts is recomputed for each beta. The portfolio is assumed to earn exactly the return that its beta implies."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:5.5#23", "source_key": "fd-textbook:5.5", "chapter": 5, "section": "5.5", "heading": "Stock index futures", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 23, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — 5.5 Stock index futures", "body": "If a hedged portfolio earns only the risk-free rate, the question arises why a manager would hedge in preference to selling the shares and investing the proceeds in Treasury bills. There are two reasons. The manager may believe that the shares in the portfolio have been well chosen and will perform better than their beta implies; the hedge removes the risk of the market as a whole and leaves the fund exposed only to the performance of its shares relative to the market. Alternatively, the manager may intend to hold the portfolio for the long term and require protection only for a short period; a futures hedge avoids the transaction costs of selling the portfolio and buying it back.\nIn practice the hedge is not as exact as in the example, because the realized return on a portfolio differs from the return implied by its beta. The difference is the portfolio's unsystematic risk, which index futures cannot remove. The more diversified the portfolio, the smaller this residual."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:5.6#24", "source_key": "fd-textbook:5.6", "chapter": 5, "section": "5.6", "heading": "Stack and roll", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 24, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — 5.6 Stack and roll", "body": "The date on which a hedge is to be lifted is sometimes later than the delivery date of every futures contract with adequate liquidity. The hedger must then roll the hedge forward: close out one contract shortly before it matures and take the same position in a contract with a later delivery date, repeating the procedure as often as required. A hedge constructed from a sequence of short-dated contracts is a stack-and-roll hedge.\nEach roll is made at whatever difference then prevails between the price of the contract being closed and the price of the contract being opened, and this difference is not known in advance. A rolled hedge is therefore exposed to basis risk at every roll, and not only when the hedge is finally lifted. It also carries the liquidity risk described in Chapter 2. Gains and losses on the futures are realized in cash at each settlement, while the gains and losses on the exposure being hedged may be realized much later. The losses of Metallgesellschaft in 1993, described in the historical note of Chapter 2, arose from a stack-and-roll hedge of long-term supply contracts.\nConcept check 5.3"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:5.6#25", "source_key": "fd-textbook:5.6", "chapter": 5, "section": "5.6", "heading": "Stack and roll", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 25, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — 5.6 Stack and roll", "body": "A fund manager with a $30,000,000 portfolio of beta 0.9 wishes to raise its beta to 1.5 for the next two months. Index futures are priced at 5,000 with a multiplier of $50. What position is required?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:5.6#26", "source_key": "fd-textbook:5.6", "chapter": 5, "section": "5.6", "heading": "Stack and roll", "kind": "notes", "ord": 26, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — 5.6 Stack and roll", "body": "Historical note\nThe statistical treatment of hedging dates from two papers published in 1960 and 1961 by Leland Johnson and Jerome Stein, who applied the portfolio theory of Harry Markowitz to a position in a commodity and its futures and obtained the variance-minimizing hedge ratio. Louis Ederington extended the analysis in 1979 to the newly introduced financial futures and proposed \\(\\rho^{2}\\) as the measure of hedging effectiveness.\nSouthwest Airlines is the case most often cited in favor of hedging by a corporation. In the years before 2008 it had hedged a large proportion of its fuel requirement at prices far below those reached during the rise in the price of oil, and its fuel cost per seat-mile was well below that of its competitors. When the oil price fell sharply in late 2008, the same hedges produced large losses, and the company reported its first quarterly losses in many years. Both outcomes were consequences of a single policy, which is the third argument of Section 5.2."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:Summary#27", "source_key": "fd-textbook:Summary", "chapter": 5, "section": "Summary", "heading": "Summary", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 27, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — Summary Summary", "body": "A party that will sell an asset hedges with a short futures position; a party that will buy hedges with a long position.\nHedging reduces the variability of cash flows from variables the company cannot predict, but its merit depends on the exposures of shareholders, on the behavior of competitors and selling prices, and on the understanding of those who review the results.\nThe effective price obtained from a hedge is \\(F_1+b_2\\): the futures price at inception plus the basis when the hedge is lifted. Basis risk arises from a mismatch of asset or of date.\nThe minimum-variance hedge ratio is \\(h^{*}=\\rho\\,\\sigma_S/\\sigma_F\\); the hedge removes the fraction \\(\\rho^{2}\\) of the variance. The number of contracts is \\(h^{*}Q_A/Q_F\\), or \\(\\hat hV_A/V_F\\) when the hedge is tailed.\nAn equity portfolio is hedged with \\(\\beta V_A/V_F\\) short index futures; its beta is changed to \\(\\beta^{*}\\) with \\((\\beta-\\beta^{*})V_A/V_F\\) contracts.\nA long-dated exposure can be hedged by rolling short-dated contracts, at the cost of basis risk at each roll and of a mismatch in the timing of cash flows."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises#28", "source_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises", "chapter": 5, "section": "Exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 28, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — Exercises Exercises", "body": "5.1 State whether a short hedge or a long hedge is appropriate for (a) a wheat farmer three months before harvest, (b) a United States company that must pay 5 million Swiss francs in four months, and (c) a fund manager who will receive $20 million in one month for investment in shares and fears that the market will rise before then.\n(a) Short: the farmer will sell wheat. (b) Long Swiss franc futures: the company will buy francs. (c) Long index futures: the manager will buy shares; this is sometimes called an anticipatory hedge.\n5.2 On 1 April an oil producer sells December crude oil futures at $74.00 to hedge 200,000 barrels to be sold in November. On 10 November the spot price is $68.50 and the December futures price is $69.20. Find the effective price received and the total revenue.\nBasis at close: \\(68.50-69.20=-0.70\\). Effective price: \\(F_1+b_2=74.00-0.70=\\$73.30\\). Check: sale at 68.50 plus futures gain \\(74.00-69.20=4.80\\) gives 73.30. Revenue: \\(73.30\\times200{,}000=\\$14{,}660{,}000\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises#29", "source_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises", "chapter": 5, "section": "Exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 29, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — Exercises Exercises", "body": "5.3 A company will buy 1,500,000 gallons of jet fuel in one month and hedges with heating oil futures (42,000 gallons per contract). The standard deviations of one-month price changes are $0.028 for jet fuel and $0.035 for the futures, and the correlation is 0.95. Find the minimum-variance hedge ratio, the number of contracts, and the hedge effectiveness.\n\\(h^{*}=0.95\\times0.028/0.035=0.76\\). \\(N^{*}=0.76\\times1{,}500{,}000/42{,}000=27.1\\); buy 27 contracts. Effectiveness: \\(0.95^{2}=0.9025\\).\n5.4 “If the minimum-variance hedge ratio is 1.0, the hedge must be perfect.” Is this statement correct?\nNo. \\(h^{*}=\\rho\\,\\sigma_S/\\sigma_F\\) equals 1.0 whenever \\(\\sigma_F=\\rho\\,\\sigma_S\\); for example \\(\\rho=0.5\\) and \\(\\sigma_F=0.5\\sigma_S\\). The hedge is perfect only if \\(\\rho=1\\), since the fraction of variance removed is \\(\\rho^{2}\\). With \\(\\rho=0.5\\) the hedge removes only 25 percent of the variance.\n5.5 A portfolio is worth $8,000,000 and has a beta of 1.2. Index futures are priced at 5,200 with a multiplier of $50. (a) What position hedges the portfolio? (b) What position reduces its beta to 0.5?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises#30", "source_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises", "chapter": 5, "section": "Exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 30, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — Exercises Exercises", "body": "\\(V_F=5{,}200\\times50=\\$260{,}000\\). (a) \\(1.2\\times8{,}000{,}000/260{,}000=36.9\\); short 37 contracts. (b) \\((1.2-0.5)\\times8{,}000{,}000/260{,}000=21.5\\); short 22 contracts (or 21; either rounding is defensible).\n5.6 In Worked example 5.3, suppose instead that the index rises by 8 percent over the three months. Find the value of the hedged position.\nIndex return with dividends: 8.3 percent. Portfolio return: \\(1+1.3\\times(8.3-1)=10.49\\) percent; value \\(12{,}000{,}000\\times1.1049=\\$13{,}258{,}800\\). Index at maturity: 6,480. Futures: \\(52\\times(6{,}042-6{,}480)\\times50=-\\$1{,}138{,}800\\). Hedged value: \\(13{,}258{,}800-1{,}138{,}800=\\$12{,}120{,}000\\), again a return of 1.0 percent.\n5.7 A copper fabricator passes every change in the price of copper to its customers within a few days. Its treasurer proposes to hedge the next twelve months of copper purchases with futures. Advise the board."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises#31", "source_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises", "chapter": 5, "section": "Exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 31, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — Exercises Exercises", "body": "The company's margin is already largely insulated from the price of copper, because its revenue and its cost move together. Fixing the cost while revenue continues to vary would create an exposure where little existed: if copper fell, selling prices would fall while the hedged cost did not, and the futures would show a loss. The only exposure worth hedging is the copper held for the few days between purchase and repricing, and inventory valued at fixed prices. The board should decline the proposal as described.\n5.8 ★ A hedger is long one unit of an asset with \\(\\sigma_S=0.50\\) and short \\(h\\) units of futures with \\(\\sigma_F=0.40\\); \\(\\rho=0.80\\). (a) Find \\(h^{*}\\) and the minimum standard deviation. (b) Find the standard deviation when \\(h=1\\) and when \\(h=2\\), and comment."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises#32", "source_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises", "chapter": 5, "section": "Exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 32, "title": "Chapter 5, Hedging with Futures — Exercises Exercises", "body": "(a) \\(h^{*}=0.80\\times0.50/0.40=1.0\\). Minimum standard deviation: \\(0.50\\sqrt{1-0.64}=0.30\\). (b) At \\(h=1\\): \\(v=0.25+0.16-2(0.8)(0.5)(0.4)=0.09\\), standard deviation 0.30, as in (a). At \\(h=2\\): \\(v=0.25+0.64-0.64=0.25\\), standard deviation 0.50, equal to the unhedged value. The variance is a parabola in \\(h\\), symmetric about \\(h^{*}\\): a hedge ratio of \\(2h^{*}\\) removes no risk at all, and any larger ratio increases it."}]$aim_json$::jsonb, 'Financial Derivatives (MacroNations textbook)');
